## Changing the config
The sandbox is configured by `{{.ConfigPath}}`. You may edit it directly.

Editing it **does not affect the current session**: in hook mode commands route
through a policy snapshot frozen at launch, and the nono profiles are generated
once when the session starts. An edit takes effect the next time the operator
runs `agent-sandbox claude`.

After editing, run `agent-sandbox ai config-check` — it loads the config and
resolves both profiles exactly as launch does, so a passing check means the
config will not be what breaks the next launch.

Schema:

```toml
tool_mode = "hook"                    # "hook" | "mcp"

[mcp]
command_output_dir = "..."            # mcp tool_mode only: where command output is written

# Host access. [sandbox.shared] reaches both the agent and sandboxed commands;
# [sandbox.agent] and [sandbox.shell] reach exactly one. Nothing is inherited
# between the two sides, so a grant reaches a side only if written where that
# side can see it. All six keys below are valid in all three sections.
[sandbox.shared]
capabilities = []                     # named bundles; see the list below
allow        = []                     # directories, read+write
read         = []                     # directories, read-only
allow_file   = []                     # single files, read+write
read_file    = []                     # single files, read-only
allow_env    = []                     # environment variable names

[sandbox.agent]
allow_commands = ["go *"]             # glob patterns that run without the command sandbox
drop_commands  = [                    # patterns refused on both host and sandbox
  { pattern = "git *", message = "optional; replaces the default refusal line" },
]

[sandbox.shell]
allow_domains = []                    # extra domains on top of the developer network profile
```

Capability names: {{range $i, $c := .Capabilities}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}`{{$c}}`{{end}}.

A user-scope config at `~/.config/agent-sandbox/config.toml` is composed with
this file: scalars from the project file win, and every list is a de-duplicated
union of the two. So editing the project file can add to a list but cannot
remove what the user-scope file contributes — if a grant you did not write shows
up in `agent-sandbox ai config-check`, it comes from there.
